.Dd May 19, 2026
.Dt BITUTILS 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SET_BIT ,
.Nm CLEAR_BIT ,
.Nm TOGGLE_BIT ,
.Nm TEST_BIT ,
.Nm CLZ ,
.Nm CTZ ,
.Nm POPCNT ,
.Nm BSR ,
.Nm POPLSB64 ,
.Nm POPMSB64 ,
.Nm LOGBITS ,
.Nm logbits_width
.Nd type-safe bitwise manipulation, counting, and debugging macros
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In bitutils.h
.Fn SET_BIT "x" "n"
.Fn CLEAR_BIT "x" "n"
.Fn TOGGLE_BIT "x" "n"
.Fn TEST_BIT "x" "n"
.Fn CLZ "x"
.Fn CTZ "x"
.Fn POPCNT "x"
.Fn BSR "x"
.Fn POPLSB64 "p"
.Fn POPMSB64 "p"
.Fn LOGBITS "x"
.Ft static inline void
.Fn logbits_width "u64 x" "int n"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The provided macros and functions perform advanced bitwise manipulation, counting, scanning, and visual debugging operations.
.Pp
The first group of macros provide standard bit-level toggling based on a bit index
.Fa n :
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fn SET_BIT "x" "n"
Sets the
.Fa n Ns -th
bit in the value
.Fa x .
.It Fn CLEAR_BIT "x" "n"
Clears the
.Fa n Ns -th
bit in the value
.Fa x .
.It Fn TOGGLE_BIT "x" "n"
Flips the state of the
.Fa n Ns -th
bit in the value
.Fa x .
.It Fn TEST_BIT "x" "n"
Returns non-zero if the
.Fa n Ns -th
bit in
.Fa x
is set, and zero otherwise.
.El
.Pp
The second group of macros relies on C11
.Ic _Generic
selection to automatically route to the correct underlying compiler built-in
(e.g.,
.Fn __builtin_clz ,
.Fn __builtin_clzl ,
.Fn __builtin_clzll )
based on whether the passed argument is an
.Vt "unsigned int" ,
.Vt "unsigned long" ,
or
.Vt "unsigned long long" .
They also utilize GNU statement expressions to guarantee single evaluation of
.Fa x ,
making them safe against side effects.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fn CLZ "x"
Count Leading Zeros. Returns the number of leading 0-bits in
.Fa x ,
starting at the most significant bit.
.It Fn CTZ "x"
Count Trailing Zeros. Returns the number of trailing 0-bits in
.Fa x ,
starting at the least significant bit.
.It Fn POPCNT "x"
Population Count. Returns the total number of 1-bits (set bits) in
.Fa x .
.It Fn BSR "x"
Bit Scan Reverse. Returns the zero-based index of the highest (most significant) set bit in
.Fa x .
.El
.Pp
The third group provides destructive bit-popping macros specifically designed for 64-bit unsigned integers:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fn POPLSB64 "p"
Takes a pointer
.Fa p
to a
.Vt uint64_t .
Identifies the least significant set bit, clears it in the underlying memory, and returns its index.
.It Fn POPMSB64 "p"
Takes a pointer
.Fa p
to a
.Vt uint64_t .
Identifies the most significant set bit, clears it in the underlying memory, and returns its index.
.El
.Pp
The final group handles visual debugging and logging of bit states:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fn LOGBITS "x"
A macro that prints the binary representation of
.Fa x
to standard output. It automatically determines the bit-width based on the size of
.Fa x .
Set bits are represented by 'X' and cleared bits by '-'. A space is inserted every 8 bits for readability, followed by a newline.
.It Fn logbits_width "x" "n"
The underlying inline function for
.Fn LOGBITS .
Prints exactly
.Fa n
bits of the 64-bit value
.Fa x ,
starting from the most significant bit of the specified width down to the 0th bit.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn TEST_BIT
returns a boolean-like integer (0 or non-zero).
.Pp
.Fn CLZ
and
.Fn CTZ
return the exact bit-width of the evaluated type (32 or 64) if
.Fa x
is exactly 0. Otherwise, they return the count of leading or trailing zeros respectively.
.Pp
.Fn POPCNT
returns the number of set bits (0 if
.Fa x
is 0).
.Pp
.Fn BSR
returns the index of the highest set bit. To avoid ambiguity with bit index 0 (which represents the value 1),
.Fn BSR
returns -1 if
.Fa x
is exactly 0.
.Pp
.Fn POPLSB64
and
.Fn POPMSB64
return the index of the cleared bit. If the value pointed to by
.Fa p
is already 0, the memory is left unmodified and both macros return -1.
.Sh CAVEATS
The type-generic macros require a compiler supporting C11
.Ic _Generic
and GCC/Clang built-in functions
.Pq Fn __builtin_* .
They strictly match against unsigned integer types. Passing signed integers will result in a compile-time error.
.Pp
The debugging function
.Fn logbits_width
uses the custom type
.Vt u64 .
This assumes that a definition such as
.Ic typedef uint64_t u64;
is available in the encompassing environment.